WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.090 align:start position:0%
 
thank you very much for being with us  and before we kind of dive into the  complexities at the trade talks  I'm just keen to ask you about a story  on the front page of them on Sunday  today which claims were seen leaked  emails from a Treasury adviser  suggesting that actually Britain doesn't  need farmers at all that we could just  import our food follow the example of  Singapore which is rich without having  its own agricultural sector so do we  need farmers well look I haven't seen  the piece I've only seen bits and pieces  on social media but I think you know  there the reference to comparing Britain  with Singapore I mean Singapore has 5  million people and doesn't have any  farmed landscape so it actually has to  import all its food whereas of course  here 75% of Britain is a farmed  landscape we have a fantastic maritime  climate in which to produce our food and  of course we have 60 plus million people  here to feed so there is a sort of moral  imperative if you like for us to be able  to produce food here in this country so  absolutely we need our farmers and of  course we're the bedrock of the largest  manufacturing sector providing all those  those raw ingredients and currently  Sophie at 60% self-sufficiency at the  same time you say there's a moral  imperative but is there a cold-hearted  economic case to say that actually  farming is new a relatively small amount  of GDP why should farmers be given tax  breaks when we could import food well  clearly there is a sort of as you say a  cold economic case but look I think you  have to look at the wider aspect of all  of this you know farms are the backbone  of rural Britain the Allied trade those  jobs that are linked to farming and as I  say were the largest manufacturing  sector surely the last thing we want to  go to is importing cheaper raw  ingredients produced to lower standards  we export our conscience and our  production and with climate change and  everything else you know I've set the  pitch set the agenda that we want to be  global leaders in climate friendly  farming there's a real opportunity for  this government to lead based on  standards and integrity and actually  putting farmers at the forefront of  delivering on climate change so we have  to see this as a knob  Trinity and comparisons with Singapore  are just completely out of touch with  where this country is given these emails  the UK of course about to go into some  reintroduce with the EU but also of  course with the US are you a bit worried  that farmers could be about to be sold  down the river here look of course we're  concerned I've been talking about  nothing else you know for the last three  years  it is absolutely paramount that we get  trade right we've asked for legislation  in the agriculture bill we've asked for  a commission really to scrutinize these  trade deals and to provide our offer for  what agri-food is going to be on the  world stage we haven't had answers to  any of those yet we still you were  interviewing George just as last weekend  this is still being discussed we await  the publication of the objectives but it  is absolutely fundamental that we do not  open up our borders effectively to cheap  raw ingredients that would be illegal to  produce here the British consumer really  values animal welfare and environmental  protection as well as food safety so  those imports have to meet the same  standards that we have here which is why  we feel we need the Commission and the  legislation in the agriculture bill  let's talk about those standards in  particular and because last month the  previous Environment Secretary Theresa  Villiers said that we won't be importing  chlorinated chicken or hormone treated  beef from the United States and you  reference my interview of George Eustace  last weekend who's taken over that brief  he said that those things are currently  illegal and the government has no plans  to change that I mean do you get a sense  that the position on things like hormone  treated beef chlorinated Trickett  chicken has been relaxing or softening  slightly look I've always seen it as you  know a negotiation if you like here you  know it's clear the people of this  country want to maintain the standards  that we have the farmers want to  maintain the standards that we have the  environmentalists want to maintain that  there is a royal shared belief well  there are many I said at our conference  last week you know this is a moral  purrs test for some in government you  know we know that not everybody in  government values that but I will  continue to make the case it is the  right thing to do and and we talked  about chlorinated chicken it's actually  not about the chlorination of chicken  that is perfectly safe to eat it's about  the production methods that sit behind  that and you know here we we have rules  we have legislation on stocking density  on veterinary advice on bedding on light  in other countries they do not have any  of those rules any of that legislation  in many countries across the world  indeed a lot of them with feedlot  systems prophylactic feeding of  antibiotics to act as a growth stimulant  we banned all of that here 30 years ago  so why would we want to go back there  this surely is the chance to lead beyond  the world stage driving standards and  integrity forwards not going back to the  dark ages undermining our farmers and  ultimately putting them out of business  I don't think anybody in government  wants to see that happen and you  mentioned there the moral compass of  some in government and an interview with  Politico earlier this week you suggested  that George Eustace might not willed  quite the same Authority perhaps or  influence as someone like Michael Gove I  don't doubt his sincerity but this is  heavyweight political discussions it  will be testing the moral compass of son  are you confident that the current  environment secretary has got the clout  to protect farmers as you'd see it when  the going gets tough look Georgie says  absolutely understands his brief inside  out he's been in the department since  2013 he's a passionate advocate of  British farming but this is you know  whoever is in his job has the same  challenge this approach has to be  hardwired across government so you know  this cannot sit with any one man indeed  any one department this has to  absolutely come from the top be  hardwired across government and every  department has to be bought into that  ambition when you last on the show and  during the conservative leadership  campaign last summer you said that you'd  written to Boris Johnson and about  consent of a nodal Brixton had heard  anything back I'm just wondering you  were saying it looked bit early that you  haven't had answers on everything from  the government has communication  proved with number Turner you having  meetings with ministers look we're  meeting regularly with different  ministers and I met with Liz trust the  Department of International Trade not  that long ago I haven't had the chance  to meet the Prime Minister yet we have  written it would be I think a  much-needed meeting to discuss the  opportunity in all of this I think we  spend a lot of time discussing the  negative aspect I really do you think  there is an enormous opportunity for  Britain to lead on the world stage the  WTO is bereft of leadership at the  moment but we have to make sure that for  my sector those those values if you like  that we hold so dear are embedded in  trade deals and of course you know we  face the prospect of a No Deal situation  if we can't agree the EU relationship we  can't agree trade deals before next  January we are back into the No Deal  prospect so you know we have to get this  right we absolutely have to make sure we  prioritize the EU relationship and I  other thing there's a huge opportunity  once we've done that with the bilateral  with the US and indeed other trade  agreements across the world okay and  just finally flooding and still of  course the affecting large parts of the  country many farms impacted are you  getting enough support look I can't tell  you how bad this is  you know we've got a third of the  country that is under water we've got  very little planting done there's been  communities rural communities urban  communities flooded we feel that farmers  are the solution in all this we  published a flood manifesto in 2012 we  re launched it again in 2016 nothing has  changed you know we are going to have to  invest in our water infrastructure  there's a great opportunity for us to be  able to store water so we pay farmers to  store water and then potentially we can  move it which the English water  companies are down talking about we can  move it to areas where we need it most  in the drier months of the year and of  course use it for irrigation  once it's stored you know we are  importing a vast amount of our fruit and  vegetables we only produce seven percent  of our fruit here there's a fantastic  opportunity for us to produce much more  fruit here and we have the water we have  a bountiful supply of  we are sourcing that fruit and vege from  from water scarce parts of the country  so I think this is an opportunity to  have a major reset on water  infrastructure and of course climate  change constantly changing weather  events more extreme we've got to do it  we've got to bite the bullet and invest  in our water infrastructure ok minute  batter's thank you very much for coming  the program this morning thank you

